WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:11.060
Welcome, my name is Martin Keene and I'm delighted to be your instructor as we explore how open

2
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:16.060
standards are implemented in a ZOS system by ZOS Unix.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.540
We're going to be looking at all aspects from this, from the far system to the shell and

4
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:26.060
beyond, and you'll get your chance to get your hands dirty with a series of lab exercises

5
00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.860
using a real IBM Z Unix system.

6
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.220
To try out the concepts that we will be covering here.

7
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.660
So let's get cracking.

8
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:42.300
Now, ZOS Unix brings together the open world and the ZOS world.

9
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.780
Both worlds can coexist independently while sharing the same processing and storage resources.

10
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.580
And it's a case of getting the best of both worlds, a standard interface and portability

11
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:59.900
from the open part of ZOS Unix and a larger rate of services from the MVS part of ZOS.

12
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:06.100
ZOS Unix supports an environment of large operating systems or servers, distributed systems and

13
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.300
workstations that share common interfaces.

14
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.060
ZOS Unix supports the set of standards being developed by the open group.

15
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:20.900
This includes the portable operating system interface better known as POSIX.

16
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.220
Users can concentrate their work in the environment where they're most comfortable, be that Unix

17
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.940
or be that MVS.

18
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.220
So for example, application developers can develop most of their applications at their

19
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:38.140
workstations and then use standard POSIX interfaces to compile, test and debug their

20
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.420
programs on the ZOS system.

21
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.860
Current ZOS applications run as usual.

22
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.060
Current users continue to have all of the services of ZOS available.

23
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:55.420
And with ZOS Unix, the existing investment in ZOS applications and data is protected.

24
00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:00.460
New applications will have access to existing data.

25
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.460
Now, what are open systems?

26
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:09.660
Well, based on requirements from customers, the characteristics of an open system environment

27
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.980
can be expressed by the following elements.

28
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.660
First off, there's portability.

29
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:22.100
This is the ability to relocate system resources such as applications, data and people between

30
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:27.700
processing environments of different design and different architectures.

31
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.940
There's also interoperability.

32
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.220
This defines the ability to interconnect systems from different vendors and have them work

33
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.820
together to solve a business problem.

34
00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:41.300
And finally, there's investment protection, which is the ability to choose components

35
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:47.380
from offerings of many vendors, many protocols and many architectures, all with the confidence

36
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.850
that they're going to work together.

37
00:02:51.850 --> 00:03:00.050
In an open system environment, a set of standards can provide the ability to interoperate and

38
00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:05.410
to port applications, data and people on different operating platforms.

39
00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.170
Now, the vendor names that we're showing here, they're just an example.

40
00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:15.570
But the point is, it's this defined set of standards in the middle that enable interoperability

41
00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.450
and portability of applications.

42
00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.410
And there are two types of standards.

43
00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:24.970
There are de facto standards and de jure standards.

44
00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:29.490
De facto standards, those are established by broad industry usage of a technology.

45
00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:35.930
And de jure standards are established and accepted by recognized standard bodies through a formal

46
00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.530
voting process.

47
00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:44.770
The single Unix specification or SUS is the collective name of a family of standards for

48
00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:50.090
computer operating systems, compliance with which is required to qualify for using the

49
00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.050
Unix trademark.

50
00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:58.170
If an operating system is submitted to the open group for certification and passes conformance

51
00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:04.170
tests, then it's deemed to be a compliant Unix standard.

52
00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:10.210
ZOS Unix meets these open systems requirements.

53
00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:17.550
Unix applications which conform to XPG 4.2 standards can be ported to ZOS.

54
00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.910
These can be vendor applications or self-developed applications.

55
00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:26.910
Now does this mean that every Unix application should run on Z?

56
00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:32.150
Well of course not, but applications that require true enterprise server qualities

57
00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.990
of service, they are well suited.

58
00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:40.590
If your application has high DASD requirements, high security needs, or needs a high level

59
00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:46.150
of reliability, then you'll see a lot of benefit in moving that application to ZOS

60
00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:47.150
Unix.

61
00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:53.110
In the next video, we'll take a look at how Unix is implemented with ZOS.
